perplexity: write the queued log lines before the exit
Fold-into: upstream/tools

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

common_log_main() does not delete its log (upstream issue 22142). Thus,
when main() returns, the worker thread of the log can hold lines that it
did not write. llama-perplexity writes the last chunk and the KL
statistics immediately before the return, thus a run with the exit status
0 can have no KL statistics (finding QT1, task #172). The KL harness then
reads no result, or a wrong one from the chunks that it has.

This patch calls common_log_flush(common_log_main()) before the return.

Measured on the x86 host on 2026-09-23, 60 runs of the KL comparison of one
toy model for each build (host load 40 to 85):

| Build | Runs without the KL statistics |
|---|---|
| debug, no sanitizer | 5 of 60 |
| debug, no sanitizer, with this patch | 0 of 60 |
| release (the shipped flags), no sanitizer | 4 of 60 |
| release, no sanitizer, with this patch | 0 of 60 |

An earlier measurement gave 12 of 60 for the native host build. Under a
high load the loss occurred in 6 runs in a row.

diff --git a/tools/perplexity/main.cpp b/tools/perplexity/main.cpp
--- a/tools/perplexity/main.cpp
+++ b/tools/perplexity/main.cpp
@@ -1,5 +1,11 @@
+#include "log.h"
+
 int llama_perplexity(int argc, char ** argv);
 
 int main(int argc, char ** argv) {
-    return llama_perplexity(argc, argv);
+    const int ret = llama_perplexity(argc, argv);
+    // common_log_main() does not delete its log (upstream issue 22142), thus its worker thread can hold the
+    // last lines at the exit. Write the queued lines (the last chunk and the KL statistics) before the return.
+    common_log_flush(common_log_main());
+    return ret;
 }
